speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, I do not want to be thought dumb but I still think that this amendment does what I suggested it does. It sets up two classes of shareholders. It says in part:
*-held by such person and by other shareholders associated with him, if any, exceeds 10 per cent of the total number of the issued and outstanding shares of such stock;
Surely this must refer to the period before the extension of the authorized capital. Those must be the shares actually issued and outstanding at the time the governor in council is to authorize the expansion of capital. In effect you are saying that the original shareholders will have their percentage of shareholdings diluted. If you double the capitalization they will be allowed to have only 5 per cent of the total but the new shareholder coming in can have 10 per cent.
